turtle shell but regardless of  appearance it's virtually impenetrable  to weapons another weak point it's belly  button few get the chance to try to kill  the monster  depending on the source it's horrific  odor or supernatural abilities disorient  would-be hunters  those that might get a lucky shot off  may not even be able to get near enough  to verify the kill because the smell  emanating from the remains is so foul it  cannot be tolerated by even those with  the strongest stomachs the first tales  of the map in guarani come from the  indigenous peoples of the amazon basin  where legends of forest creatures abound  in the amazon across the states of  rodonia amazonas akre and para the map  ngwari is part of folklore and reported  in eyewitness accounts alike brazil is  home to more than about 300 indigenous  groups with great linguistic diversity  with upwards of 150 languages across so  many communities it's typical to find  variations in this monster's name or  story  it is difficult to be sure about the  etymology of the name mapingary but the  main assumption is that it comes from  the tupiguarini indigenous language  meaning a thing with the twisted feet  or  that who has a twisted feet  that's dr andreoli costa folklore  researcher and consultant he's an expert  in brazilian folklore and a great  storyteller to boot  the first reports of the mapingwary myth  date from the turn of the 20th century  and coinciding with the rubber cycle in  the amazon  and it's precisely among the rubber  tappers where we find some of the oldest  stories about the mapingwary other  monsters share characteristics with the  map in guari like the segamai an  herbivore about the size of a cow with  matted fur who can walk on all fours or  its back two legs emits a blood-curdling  roar and cannot be harmed by regular  weapons but has a snout more like that  of a giant anteater then there's the  protective kurupira the feet which we  mentioned earlier are sometimes turned  backwards like the brazilian chlorophyll  and other forest beings who use this  strategy to confuse their pursuers  in others which are the most common  today they say the footprints of the  mapiguri are round like the hand of a  pesto or a bottle  or the bottom of a bottle  like another brazilian meat  called bottle foot  papinberry has a vertical gigantic mouth  in its abdomen  a very similar mouth to another myth  a traditional narrative among the  enslaved africans in brazil  the difference is that the kibongo's  mouth is in the back there are stories  in which the mapingworthy has both eyes  but often they say it has only one  which underlines its bestiality the one  eye the cyclope guy  refers to bestiality the uncontrolled  violence  of those who cannot distinguish  right and wrong  it's tempting to use the similarities of  the mapaguari myth across regional  traditions to connect the creature to an  animal one that lived not so long ago or  even one still alive today  this is where the most popular and  notorious rationalization for the map  inquiry comes into play the giant sloth  theory which claims the map inquiry and  other similar creatures are folkloric  interpretations of a real but long  extinct animal  though in the spirit of other elusive  monsters there are some people who chase  legends to prove their existence  in the 19th century written claims  emerged from south america proclaiming  that members of the milo don today  ground sloth family believed to be  extinct were actually alive and well  argentinian paleontologist florentino a  megino publicly asserted he believed  giant ground sloths still lived in the  patagonia region even in the 21st  century the mapping guardi as a  groundslaw theory is enthusiastically  supported perhaps no more publicly than  biologist and ornithologist dr david  oren  oren has dedicated a large portion of  his life to proving the continued  existence of prehistoric ground sloths  he asserts that the monster is the  result 